{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Now you shall see what I will do to Pharaoh.</b> The word ״עַתָּה״ is a proper response to Moses for having said, ״לָמָה הֲרֵעֹתָה לָעָם הַזֶּה״ (Exodus 5:22) — for Moses did not complain about the fact that Pharaoh did not send Israel forth immediately upon his arrival, since the Holy One, blessed be He, had already told him twice that the king of Egypt would not let them go. Rather, he complained about the fact that it grew worse for this people than at first: for at the very least, if his mission would not benefit them immediately, it should not harm them — and yet our eyes see that it harmed them through his mission. And Moses said: ״Perhaps I was the cause of this, for You sent to him a man heavy of mouth and tongue like me, and therefore Pharaoh's anger grew especially fierce, for he said, 'See, the Jews are toying with me' — and if so, why did You send me?״ And perhaps he understood this from the words of Pharaoh, who said ״לָמָה מֹשֶׁה וְאַהֲרֹן תַּפְרִיעוּ אֶת הָעָם״ (Exodus 5:4) — for he should have said, 'Moses and Aaron, why do you disturb?' Rather, thus he said: 'If some other suitable man and speaker had undertaken this mission to disturb the people from their work, I would have held my peace. But why Moses and Aaron, who are not fit for this mission — they will disturb the people from their labors?' This angered him all the more, that he should be sent a despicable emissary rather than one worthy of the mission." "And the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him: ״עַתָּה תִרְאֶה״ etc. (Exodus 6:1). For it is well known that such is the natural order: every day, just before dawn, the darkness grows deeper than the darkness of night, and afterward the light of dawn breaks through and rises. And so too most sick people, near the time of their death, grow stronger and sit up in bed and ask to eat, and afterward death overcomes them. And likewise in the days of winter, near the rising of the sun, the cold grows ever stronger, and in the end it is defeated by the sun. And this is a natural thing, for every natural entity that senses the approach of some contrary force seeking to annul its existence, it then strengthens itself all the more against its adversary and refuses to yield before it, acting according to its nature with all the power it can muster, until in the end it is defeated, for its opposite has prevailed over it. So it is with all the things we have mentioned. Likewise, the fact that Pharaoh made things worse for Israel now than he had done before — this is a decisive sign that his end is drawing near and that the time of redemption is close, to annul all of Pharaoh's deeds; therefore he seeks to strengthen himself through his deeds. This is what He said: ״עַתָּה תִרְאֶה״ — with the word ״עַתָּה״ He answers him regarding what he asked, ״לָמָה הֲרֵעֹתָה״ etc. (Exodus 5:22), because now the time has arrived when he will be compelled to send them forth and drive them out; therefore he seeks to make war upon them with a strong hand. And this is a sign, for ״קָרוֹב יִשְׁעִי לָבוֹא וְצִדְקָתִי לְהִגָּלוֹת״ (Isaiah 56:1)." "Also, one may explain the phrase ״עַתָּה תִרְאֶה״ as indicating that the decree of four hundred years that they were to spend in Egypt had not yet arrived — for they had been enslaved only רד״ו [210] years. Therefore He added trouble and servitude for them, so that the small measure would be reckoned as a larger one, for in these few days it is as though they were enslaved in them many years; and through this the end would draw near to them, so that ״עַתָּה״ — immediately — ״תִרְאֶה אֵת אֲשֶׁר אֶעֱשֶׂה לְפַרְעֹה״. And this is a precise reading."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {2 "The abbreviation רד״ו represents the numerical value 210 (ר=200, ד=4, ו=6), the traditional count of years Israel was actually enslaved in Egypt."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:35.339955113Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}